diff --git a/docs/fork-touchpoints.md b/docs/fork-touchpoints.md
index 27eed6311..490e2cf29 100644
--- a/docs/fork-touchpoints.md
+++ b/docs/fork-touchpoints.md
@@ -4257,3 +4257,14 @@ The emitted lights and the standalone library shaders keep their physical illumi
   lighting without camera extinction. The composite, rainbow and PSR resolver use this
   surface for hazed misses. The G-buffer and composite binding indices, resource
   manager and `rtx_numos.cpp` carry both reflected-ground records to the post-depth air.
+
+### Enhanced No Horizon
+
+- `rtx_fork_numos_horizon.h` reflects both cameras and selects the second renderer's
+  settings. `rtx_numos.cpp` owns both the renderer and the lower sky-view table.
+  `rtx_numos_gpu.*` reports the main sky-view dispatch and `rtx_numos_ui.cpp`
+  exposes the shared option on the Enhanced Sky page.
+- `numos_horizon_air.comp.slang` keeps the pin's quadrature and night sources with
+  independent march and phase directions. The common/composite bindings, sky
+  provider, visible-disc helper and composite carry the lower atmosphere, clouds,
+  motion and haze to camera misses and specular and indirect gathers.
diff --git a/documentation/NumosSky.md b/documentation/NumosSky.md
index 02ff6637a..3b275f2a2 100644
--- a/documentation/NumosSky.md
+++ b/documentation/NumosSky.md
@@ -197,3 +197,28 @@ distance and coloured throughput separately for each compacted path. After depth
 composite integrates that segment through the current air cache, adds the lit ground
 and the cloud dome, and applies the camera-to-mirror air. With haze off the ordinary
 NUMOS sky is used. Indirect sky lighting keeps the library's own ground.
+
+With **No Horizon** selected, the eye-level lower hemisphere uses a 512 × 128
+RGBA16F table. Its texels are the lower rows of NUMOS's 512 × 256 sky view.
+The path length and transmission use the mirrored elevation, while the Sun,
+each moon, zodiacal light and celestial catalogue use the actual sightline.
+The table is baked on first use and whenever the main renderer bakes its sky
+view. The image night gets its usual stellar in-scatter correction. Both
+halves sample their own side of the horizon when filtering the final texel.
+
+Camera misses see a second NUMOS renderer's screen clouds in the opposite
+direction. Both camera rotations are point-reflected about their respective
+eyes, retaining view-space depth and jitter. The renderer starts each frame
+with the main wind, evolution and clocks and the measured ground. It uses
+all-sky depth, with aerial perspective, probes, lightning, secondary dome and
+night assets disabled. Its clouds are converted to the main pre-exposure.
+Motion follows a cloud's positive apparent depth and the previous eye's
+reflection point. Real surfaces use the main depth-clipped clouds and air.
+
+Across the last half degree the camera and dome clouds fade into the shared
+horizon air. The real near field and its local lights give way to mirrored
+far air over two degrees below eye level. At steeper angles the mirrored
+height fog starts at the host volumetric handoff. The night image, meteors,
+catalogue stars and lit moons retain their celestial directions. The Sun and
+its corona set. Secondary rays use the main cloud dome at the antipode.
+Disabling the option releases the second renderer and lower table.
